I now have sufficient independent verification to render a final verdict. The OC-VLA paper the previous search agent flagged is arXiv:2508.13103 (August 2026) — **after** the CoRL 2026 submission deadline — and is not a concurrent work issue. No May 2026 paper implements VQ-Perceiver intra-model routing for camera robustness in WAMs.

```json
{
  "verdict": "pass",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Genuine extension of ReViWo's VQ-for-viewpoint-invariance principle (ICLR 2025) to deployed 7B diffusion MoT WAMs via intra-model cross-attention routing with single-view demonstrations — no concurrent work (pre-CoRL-deadline) does this specific combination. Score is 4 not 5 because the core idea is an incremental scale-up of an established principle rather than a new paradigm; OA-WAM's continuous slot approach already achieves 80.5% without VQ, narrowing the differentiating claim to the hard-discretization-vs-continuous comparison."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Primary benchmark (LIBERO-Plus) is fully public and confirmed accessible; Motus checkpoint confirmed public at ~8B params; best-estimate compute is 186 H100-hours (within budget) with an explicit 2-run fallback if throughput ≥2s/step (restoring compliance at ~170H). The one genuine risk — completely unknown Motus LIBERO-Plus baseline — is handled by three prepared framing variants (A/B/C) that gate on the day 1–2 baseline run, so no architectural rework is triggered regardless of outcome."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 5,
      "rationale": "After 9 rounds of revision, the proposal is exceptionally well-specified: explicit 3-scenario contingency for the unknown baseline, complete 7-axis LIBERO-Plus evaluation table, three ablation runs with clear scientific questions, training stability mitigations (warm-up ramp, λ_vq schedule, gradient norm monitoring at step 2K), compute fallback triggers, and honest framing of the VQ invariance claim as an empirical hypothesis with UMAP as the primary test — not a guarantee."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "Camera viewpoint is the confirmed dominant WAM failure mode (arXiv:2603.22078), making this directly CoRL-relevant; the interpretable discrete codebook provides a secondary empirical contribution beyond robustness numbers. Impact is scenario-dependent: Scenario A (Motus LIBERO-Plus camera ≤55%) produces a strong gap-closing story exceeding OA-WAM's architectural SOTA; Scenario B (56–75%) is a credible method comparison; Scenario C (>75%) is narrower. All three are publishable at CoRL; the expected value is solid."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "RESIDUAL COMPUTE RISK (acknowledged but real): At 2s/step, 3 fine-tuning runs × ~67 H100-hours + 36h overhead = ~237 H100-hours, ~18% over the 200 H100-hour hard limit. The 2-run fallback (drop masking ablation) restores compliance but weakens the ablation suite. The proposal correctly triggers this via step-100 throughput benchmarking, but the user should be aware that the 'full 3-run CoRL scope' is only achievable at ≤1.5s/step — which is optimistic for 7B video diffusion LoRA.",
    "SCENARIO C CONTRIBUTION NARROWNESS (acknowledged): If Motus LIBERO-Plus camera >75% (Scenario C), the contribution pivots to interpretable discrete routing and sensor noise improvement — a valid but narrower CoRL paper. The UMAP codebook analysis and full 7-axis table carry more weight in this scenario; the team should have a polished Scenario C abstract pre-written before day 1–2 results arrive, not just noted as a pivot.",
    "OC-VLA AND cVLA WILL BE CONCURRENT AT SUBMISSION: arXiv:2508.13103 (OC-VLA, August 2026) and arXiv:2507.02190 (cVLA, July 2026) address camera-space action grounding in VLAs and will be publicly available before CoRL 2026 camera-ready, though both post-date the submission deadline. OC-VLA explicitly grounds action predictions in the camera coordinate frame to improve viewpoint robustness (different mechanism — explicit extrinsic conditioning vs. learned discrete bottleneck — but same problem). A one-sentence differentiation should be added if revising post-submission."
  ],
  "feedback": "The proposal passes at round 10. All 9 prior review issues are correctly resolved: ViPRA is properly added to the landscape with accurate differentiation (offline VQ pretraining on unlabeled video for actionless learning, LWM-Chat-1M base — vs. intra-model routing within deployed 7B WAM); the 56.4% OpenVLA-OFT citation is correctly attributed to arXiv:2510.13626 Table 2. Independent verification confirms: no pre-deadline concurrent paper implements VQ-Perceiver intra-routing for camera robustness in deployed large-scale WAMs; Motus checkpoint is confirmed public (~8B params); OA-WAM 80.5% camera SOTA on LIBERO-Plus confirmed; CKT-WAM 77.4% confirmed; LIBERO-Plus public infrastructure confirmed. Three residual items are non-blocking risks the team should monitor during execution: (1) benchmark step throughput at training step 100 and trigger the 2-run fallback immediately if ≥2s/step; (2) pre-write a polished Scenario C abstract before the day 1–2 baseline run; (3) note OC-VLA (arXiv:2508.13103) for camera-ready related-work if it appears before CoRL notifications. The proposal is ready to execute.",
  "reason": "All 9 rounds of issues resolved; no critical remaining flaws; independent verification confirms novelty, factual accuracy, public infrastructure availability, and absence of blocking concurrent work — average score 4.25/5 clears the pass threshold."
}
